+Power Ball (prototype)
+Kong Ball (prototype, TCH / Digital Dreams Multimedia)
+
+Original driver by Tomasz Slanina.
+The video model, the blitter and their confirmation against the machine by Marcos
+Hernández, in collaboration, within MS-DOS Club's TCH preservation project
+(github.com/MS-DOS-Club/tch-blitter).
+
+THE BOARD (TCH E133): two MC68HC000FN16 at 16 MHz, two Actel TPC1020BFN FPGAs that
+are the scaling blitter, a BT478 RAMDAC, an ADC0808 for the wheel and the pedals, a
+ST93C46 EEPROM, one 32 MHz crystal, twelve TMS27C040, one GAL16V8QS and two GAL22V10,
+a JAMMA edge and a VGA connector that links two cabinets. The blitter paints two
+512x512 planes: a background torus that scrolls, with a per-line vertical scroll for
+the road, and a sprite plane of two 256-line pages, one displayed while the game
+draws into the other.
+
+THE INTERRUPTS of the main 68000:
+
+- level 1: the blitter, a HELD level, up while the blitter is idle and when a blit
+  completes, down at the arming write.
+- level 3: vblank.
+- level 5: the line counter the game loads at 0x7a0000, counting only while
+  bit 13 of 0x7c0000 enables it. It clocks the sound streamer on all four games
+  (one word per line to the sub cpu), and in Wheels & Fire the same handler also
+  writes the road's per-line scroll.
+
+THE BLITTER, read from the games' own code and measured in the emulator:
+
+- A command is armed with 0x0000 to word 15 and fired with 0xffff. The endpoints
+  are inclusive at 1:1; a zoomed blit stops one step short.
+- The game paces the chain with its own interrupt mask (#$2000 opens it, #$2100
+  closes it), one blit per interrupt.
+- Transparency is a REGISTER, not a flag: a write to the low byte of word 12
+  selects transparent and to word 13 opaque (the games write 0xff, one of them as a
+  word), and the arming write resets it, so a command that writes neither draws
+  transparent, which is the normal case.
+- Word 14 marks a FILL: the command clears the band of rows it declares, edge to
+  edge of the plane, to index 0, its height counted on the 512-row torus. The game
+  double-buffers with one fill of half a page per frame and clears a whole plane
+  between screens with a 512-row one.
+- Word 7 bit 2 reads the source and bit 3 also writes index 0: both together are a
+  colour fill, bit 3 alone an ERASE that walks its destination in columns.
+- Word 7 bits 0 and 1 steer the destination walk and word 8 bits 14 and 15 the
+  source walk; a mirror is a backward destination under a forward source.
+- The zoom is a pair of stall counters. The percentage is only the index of a
+  16-byte descriptor in the program ROM, and the source column of each destination
+  step comes from that descriptor's two tables and shift. The scale fields the
+  blitter receives are laid out in the register map of blit_w.
+- The first source row of a 1:1 blit is a transparent border the art carries; the
+  driver skips it at 1:1 only, where stacked strips showed it as a seam.
+- No bit of the block sampled at a trigger, and no write to it in the three games,
+  behaves as a plane enable: both planes are always composed. The two low bits of
+  word 8 seen a few dozen times per attract are the line handler running before
+  its table pointer is set, copying a ROM byte.
+
+THE PLANES:
+
+- The background wraps on both axes. Its 9-bit scroll is latched when the game
+  writes the Y byte (word 11), which always follows the X; bit 8 of X (word 8) is
+  live, because the scenery handler rewrites the control byte without the
+  coordinates. The plane is read three rows ahead of the scroll, a hypothesis with
+  an exact fit and no mechanism read (see screen_update).
+- The sprite plane wraps on both axes, as the background does: Radical Enduro, on the
+  same board, declares its left-edge strips from x 505 and needs the 9-bit counter to
+  carry them onto the screen, and y is the same counter. The displayed half is bit
+  0x20 of word 8 sampled on line 0 of the frame, the one moment when only the code
+  that knows has written it.
+- A blit naming a page above the ROM opens the direct-write channel: it defines a
+  window and draws nothing, and the low byte of word 6 then streams pixels into it.
+
+The window is 336x252: the game clips its sprites against 336x256 and the last four
+rows do not leave the monitor. The field is derived from the 32 MHz crystal, 512
+pixels per line and 312 lines: the 15625 Hz line rate the sound mixer counts against.
 
